In this lesson we'll look at spheres and how to tackle composite solids, which are just standard shapes joined together. First up, a sphere is every point that's the same distance r from a centre, and the formulas are beautifully simple. Volume is four-thirds pi times r cubed, and surface area is four pi r squared. Then a hemisphere is just half a sphere, so you halve the volume to get two-thirds pi r cubed. For curved surface area, it's two pi r squared, and if you include the flat circular face, the total is three pi r squared. Finally, composite solids. The trick is the same as before: add the volumes of the parts, or subtract if something has been cut away. For surface area, only count the faces that are actually showing, because any joined surfaces disappear. So picture a cone sitting on top of a cylinder. You add the cylinder's volume to the cone's volume, but for surface area you only count the bottom circle, the cylinder's side, and the cone's slant. Now let's see it in action.